The problem asks us to calculate the sum of the "power" of all non-empty subgroups of heroes.
The power of a group of heroes with values $v_1, v_2, \dots, v_k$ is defined as $(\max(v_1, \dots, v_k))^2 \times (\min(v_1, \dots, v_k))$.
We need to return the sum of these powers modulo $10^9 + 7$.

Let the array be $nums$. The length of $nums$ is up to $10^5$. The values in $nums$ are up to $10^9$.
A brute-force approach would be to iterate over all non-empty subarrays (or subsets). Since the order of indices matters for the definition of a group (though the problem says "group of heroes", usually in such problems, it implies a subset of indices, but let's check the example).
Example 1: nums = [2, 1, 4].
Groups listed:
[2] -> indices {0}
[1] -> indices {1}
[4] -> indices {2}
[2, 1] -> indices {0, 1}
[2, 4] -> indices {0, 2}
[1, 4] -> indices {1, 2}
[2, 1, 4] -> indices {0, 1, 2}
